{
  "dom_id": "step-q117170",
  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14255079/assets/baebfa372080454c292fc03af86b932fef00a688e9174ffcd49f80c6b009f7dc.png)\n\nThe graph above shows one branch of the curve $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x+C)$ for some number $C,$ where $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ and a vertical asymptote of the curve. What is the equation of the vertical asymptote?",
  "worked_solution": "Let's add the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ to the plot.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14255079/assets/65fc7e1c131bc729022173c652429574719466b10b0ab465c0a5981da77d4228.png)\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$\n\n- Then, shift it by $\\frac{1}{4}$ units **left.**\n\nTherefore, the given graph is $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x+\\frac{1}{4}),$ and so $C=\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nSince the graph is shifted by $\\frac{1}{4}$ units left, the asymptote is also shifted by $\\frac{1}{4}$ units left. The corresponding asymptote on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Therefore, the equation of the asymptote shown is\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{2\\pi -1}{4}.\n$$",
